WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:02.410
- [Reporter] President Biden has nominated

00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:04.050
Judge Ketanji Brown Jackson

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:05.440
to the Supreme Court,
- Good afternoon.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.620
- the first black woman
nominated to be a justice

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.370
on the nation's highest court.

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:12.220
If confirmed, she would
replace Justice Stephen Breyer,

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:13.510
a liberal member of the court

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:15.690
who announced his retirement in January.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:17.530
Judge Jackson served as a law clerk

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:19.730
to Justice Breyer earlier in her career.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:22.470
- I've been a federal
judge for eight years

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:27.400
and I have a duty of independence.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:32.110
I clerked for three federal
judges before I became a judge

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:36.900
and they were models of
judicial independence,

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:41.180
and what that has meant
is that I know very well

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.240
what my obligations
are, what my duties are,

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:50.240
not to rule with partisan
advantage in mind.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:52.440
- What makes her a
strong nominee, in part,

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.230
is that professional diversity.

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.760
This sort of allows Biden
to fulfill a promise

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.950
that he's made to diversify the court,

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.010
not just by putting people in the court

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:04.110
who might have a different
demographic background,

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.490
who might look different,
but people who might also

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:09.010
just bring a different kind of experience.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:11.140
Another thing that makes
her a strong nominee

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:14.610
is that she was just recently
confirmed to the DC Circuit,

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:15.950
and so it means that she's gone

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.760
through the Senate confirmation process.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.260
- [Reporter] The president
made his decision

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:20.680
after meeting with the candidates,

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.650
studying their histories and case records,

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.210
and consulting legal experts,
the White House said.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.000
- Although we have a pretty
closely divided Senate,

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.340
we think she still probably
has a pretty smooth path

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:32.173
to confirmation.

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:37.290
Vice President Harris
casts the deciding vote

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:40.000
and gives Democrats the majority.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.270
- [Reporter] Mr. Biden's nomination

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:42.640
won't change the balance of power

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.040
on a conservative high court,

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:47.120
that is six justices appointed
by Republican presidents,

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.990
including three by former
President Donald Trump.

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:52.910
Judge Jackson currently serves
on the US Court of Appeals

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:54.630
for the District of Columbia Circuit,

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.550
one of the nation's
most influential courts

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.530
and often a stepping stone
for Supreme Court justices.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:02.360
She joined the court last June
after spending eight years

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.810
as a federal trial judge
in the nation's capital.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:06.720
- So President Biden has promised

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.770
to diversify the federal judiciary

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:11.710
in terms of both demographic background

00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:14.490
and professional diversity,

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:17.650
and Judge Jackson ticks
both of those boxes.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:20.250
She'll be the first
black woman on the court

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:24.450
and she also brings a background
as a criminal defender,

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:27.750
which we have not seen
in many, many years.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:32.680
- I would say that my different
professional background

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.020
than many of the Court of Appeals judges,

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:38.220
including my District Court background,

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.550
which will be different, if I'm confirmed,

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:45.510
than many of my my
colleagues, would bring value.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:48.230
It's sort of like the
Oliver Wendell Holmes quote,

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:52.570
that "The life of the law is
not logic; it's experience."

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:57.570
And so I've experienced life
in perhaps a different way

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:00.590
than some of my colleagues
because of who I am,

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:03.260
and that might be valuable.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:06.590
I hope it would be valuable if
I was confirmed to the court.

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:08.740
- Republicans have tried to pin her down

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:12.320
on this question of the,
quote-unquote, living Constitution,

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.750
the idea that constitutional
interpretation

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:18.900
should adapt to social change,
should evolve over time.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.659
She had said in 2013 at
a confirmation hearing

00:03:22.659 --> 00:03:25.620
for the District Court that
she disagreed with that.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:26.590
But more recently,

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:29.280
at her Appeals Court confirmation hearing,

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.290
she really just declined to talk about it

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:33.120
and deferred to the Supreme Court.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:36.203
- The Constitution is
an enduring document.

00:03:38.025 --> 00:03:43.025
It has, the Supreme Court
has said, a fixed meaning,

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:47.060
that we are to look to the original words

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:51.120
in the Constitution and interpret,

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.210
as lower court judges,
we interpret provisions

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.941
the way in which the Supreme Court does,

00:03:56.941 --> 00:03:59.420
and they look at the text and meaning.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:01.320
- Judge Jackson has not said very much

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.670
about what kind of a presence
she will be on the court,

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:07.860
as is common with lower court judges.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:11.080
We don't know very much about
her judicial philosophy,

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:12.130
and when she was asked about it

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:14.050
at a recent confirmation hearing,

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:16.460
she really declined to
talk very much about it

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:18.433
and deferred to the Supreme Court.

00:04:18.433 --> 00:04:21.430
- I think that I would
like to be remembered

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:26.430
as a judge who was both careful
and thorough in my opinions.

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:29.020
- [Reporter] It remains unclear

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:31.540
exactly when Judge Jackson's
confirmation hearings

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:33.900
will begin, but New York
Senator Chuck Schumer

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.203
said they will take place
in the coming weeks.

